#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# asymptotic_solve

#% text
# `asymptotic_solve(eqs, [y, y0], [x, x0, k])` gives the solution y(x) of the equations with `y(x0) = y0` as a power series in `x - x0` to order k, as `[[y -> series]]`.

#% text
# `asymptotic_solve(eqs, [y], [x, x0, k])` starts from each root of the equations at x0; `asymptotic_solve(eqs, [[y1, y2], [a1, a2]], [x, x0, k])` solves a system.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The series comes from Newton's iteration on truncated power series, which needs an invertible Jacobian in the unknowns at the starting point. For one polynomial equation with a singular starting point, or branches that go to infinity (the degree in y drops at x0), every branch is given by Newton's polygon as a series in fractional powers (Puiseux series); for one equation analytic at a given singular start, the branches through it come from the polygon of its Taylor polynomial. For two polynomial equations in two unknowns with a singular Jacobian, each unknown's branches are those of the resultant that eliminates the other, and a pair is kept when it solves both equations to order k; each series keeps its terms up to (x - x0)^k. In other such cases the answer is a failure, since a list without those branches would be incomplete. With `reals`, only the real branches are given.

#% item
# `direction: from_below` gives the expansion for x < x0, in powers of x0 - x (with `reals`, the branches real there).

#% item
# When x enters the equations only through log(x), at x0 = 0 or `inf`, the solution is a series in 1/log(x) to order k.

#% item
# `asymptotic_solve(eqs, [y, y0], [[x1, x2], [a1, a2], k])` expands in several variables at a regular point: the terms of total degree up to k in x1 - a1, x2 - a2.

#% item
# Order of the branches: through regular starting points, in the order of the roots at x0 as `solve` lists them; by Newton's polygon, by the exponent of the leading power of x - x0 (bounded branches first, then those that grow faster), and among equal exponents by the real part, then the imaginary part of the leading coefficient.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
asymptotic_solve(exp(y) - cos(x*y) + x == 0, [y, 0], [x, 0, 5])
#% output
#= [[y -> -x - (1/2)*x^2 - (1/3)*x^3 - (3/4)*x^4 - (6/5)*x^5]]

#% cell
asymptotic_solve(y^2 - 1 - x == 0, [y], [x, 0, 3])
#% output
#= [[y -> -1 - (1/2)*x + (1/8)*x^2 - (1/16)*x^3], [y -> 1 + (1/2)*x - (1/8)*x^2 + (1/16)*x^3]]

#% cell
asymptotic_solve(y^3 - x*y == 24, [y, 3], [x, 1, 3])
#% output
#= [[y -> 3 - (3/17576)*(-1 + x)^2 - (309/5940688)*(-1 + x)^3 + (3/26)*(-1 + x)]]

#% cell
asymptotic_solve(sin(t + x) + cos(y) == t + 1 and log(1 + x + y) - sin(t) == 0, [[x, y], [0, 0]], [t, 0, 4])
#% output
#= [[x -> (1/2)*t^2 + (1/6)*t^3 + (1/24)*t^4, y -> t - (1/6)*t^3 - (1/6)*t^4]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# An analytic equation through a singular start: two branches in powers of x^(1/2):

#% cell
asymptotic_solve(sin(x*y) - y^2 + x == 0, [y, 0], [x, 0, 3])
#% output
#= [[y -> -x^(1/2) + (1/2)*x - (1/8)*x^(3/2) + (1/128)*x^(5/2)], [y -> x^(1/2) + (1/2)*x + (1/8)*x^(3/2) - (1/128)*x^(5/2)]]

#% text
# The real branch for x < 0 only:

#% cell
asymptotic_solve(y^5 - x*y + x^2 == 0, [y, 0], [x, 0, 5], reals, direction: from_below)
#% output
#= [[y -> x + x^4]]

#% text
# Two polynomial equations with a singular Jacobian at the start: four branches in powers of t^(1/4):

#% cell
asymptotic_solve(y^2 - t*x + t == 0 and x^2 + t*y == 0, [[x, y], [0, 0]], [t, 0, 2])
#% output
#= [[x -> -im/4*t^(3/2) + (-1)^(1/4)*t^(3/4), y -> -im*t^(1/2) + (1/2)*(-1)^(3/4)*t^(5/4)], [x -> -im/4*t^(3/2) - (-1)^(1/4)*t^(3/4), y -> -im*t^(1/2) - (1/2)*(-1)^(3/4)*t^(5/4)], [x -> (im/4)*t^(3/2) + (-1)^(3/4)*t^(3/4), y -> im*t^(1/2) + (1/2)*(-1)^(1/4)*t^(5/4)], [x -> (im/4)*t^(3/2) - (-1)^(3/4)*t^(3/4), y -> im*t^(1/2) - (1/2)*(-1)^(1/4)*t^(5/4)]]

#% text
# A series in 1/log(x) as x -> 0:

#% cell
asymptotic_solve(sin(y) - y^2 + cos(y)/log(x) == 0, [y, 0], [x, 0, 3])
#% output
#= [[y -> -5/(3*log(x)^3) - log(x)^-1 + log(x)^-2]]

#% text
# Two variables, to total degree 3:

#% cell
asymptotic_solve(z^5 - 2*z^3 + 2*z + x^2 - y == 0, [z, 0], [[x, y], [0, 0], 3])
#% output
#= [[z -> (-1/2)*x^2 + (1/8)*y^3 + (1/2)*y]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `solve`, `series`, `implicit_d`
